WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.810
Why is the Iron Throne suddenly being so violent?

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:07.839
Does this chair have a mind of its own or
is Viserys just accident-prone?

00:00:07.839 --> 00:00:10.090
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:13.410
King Viserys Targaryen is a pretty clumsy
guy, isn't he?

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:18.450
Sure, he's a mighty ruler, the patriarch of
Westeros' finest family, and a builder of

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:19.450
scale models.

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:23.310
However, the guy can't seem to sit on the
Iron Throne without inflicting serious and

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:28.119
wildly infectious cuts on himself — which
seems like a pretty strange character flaw,

00:00:28.119 --> 00:00:31.580
considering that the seat is a very central
part of his job.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:35.730
After all, pretty much everyone who sat on
the Iron Throne in Game of Thrones could lounge

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:39.899
on it any way they wanted without filleting
their muscles or appendages.

00:00:39.899 --> 00:00:41.950
As the people who are familiar with George
R.R.

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:46.780
Martin's source material may already suspect,
there's a reason behind this dramatic change

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:51.079
in the Iron Chair's effects on the person
who sits on it, and the reason goes far beyond

00:00:51.079 --> 00:00:53.809
Viserys Targaryen's personal sitting skills.

00:00:53.809 --> 00:00:57.510
In Game of Thrones, the dangers associated
with the Iron Throne tend to stem from the

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:01.520
fact that if you sit on it, several factions
will attempt to remove you from it by any

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:02.600
means necessary.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:03.859
However, George R.R.

00:01:03.859 --> 00:01:08.040
Martin's A Song of Ice and Fire series depicts
a very different Iron Throne.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:13.130
The throne was designed by none other than
Aegon I Targaryen, aka the Conqueror.

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:16.970
Being a tough enough guy to earn a nickname
like "The Conqueror" in this universe, Aegon

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:21.150
wanted to make sure that whatever rulers came
after him would be up to the task.

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:25.360
The Iron Throne was created as a combination
of a stealth test and a method for weeding

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:26.659
out the unworthy.

00:01:26.659 --> 00:01:30.920
It's uncomfortable, sharp, and dangerous,
so if you're not as vigilant as a ruler should

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:35.450
be, it's very quick to remind you in the shape
of a cut or maybe five.

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:38.610
Because of the Throne's tendency to cut the
ruler who doesn't have their head screwed

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:43.040
on right, Martin's lore includes mentions
of several kings who didn't respect the throne.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.200
The most notorious one would probably be one
Maegor Targaryen, who was so bad at sitting

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.770
that the Iron Throne actually killed him.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:53.670
Another name worth mentioning is "The Mad
King" Aerys II, who was eventually killed

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:58.490
by Jaime Lannister only after he had injured
himself by slouching on the throne.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:02.310
Even the reigning awful king, Joffrey Baratheon,
cuts himself in the books.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:07.840
It definitely appears that Viserys Targaryen
isn't the kind of guy the throne approves, either.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.440
Episode 1 of House of the Dragon includes
what seems to be an example of correct Iron

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:12.560
Throne posture.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.510
When Viserys banishes Daemon near the end
of Episode 1, he's sitting straight as an

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:18.720
arrow, wielding a sword of his own.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.890
Daemon walks away, and Viserys immediately
cuts his finger on the throne.

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:25.590
Add that to the back injury he sustained before
the beginning of the series, and it's not

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:28.790
hard to suspect that the character might not
see the end of the show.

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:32.430
The dangerous nature of the Iron Throne is
reflected in its design.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:36.629
It's difficult for a large seat made of swords
to be unassuming, but that's exactly what

00:02:36.629 --> 00:02:40.250
the original show version is compared to the
new House of the Dragon Iron Throne.

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:42.819
"There aren't a thousand blades.

00:02:42.819 --> 00:02:46.740
There aren't even two hundred.

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:48.040
I've counted."

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:52.390
This pointy, jutty thing surrounded by scores
of blades is not the kind of thing you'd imagine

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:57.069
hauling on the convention circuit for fans
to take selfies in unless they're very, very

00:02:57.069 --> 00:02:58.379
careful fans.

00:02:58.379 --> 00:03:02.280
After all, the House of the Dragon Iron Throne
is even dangerous on set, because there are

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:05.050
real weapons incorporated in its design.

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:09.110
Still, while closer to Martin's vision than
the original, even the new seat is a far cry

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:11.120
from the Iron Throne of the books.

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:16.650
The book version is an asymmetrical, dragon-sized
mountain of bent and tormented blades.

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:21.110
The House of the Dragon version still isn't
quite there, but its bulky, dangerous-looking

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:24.870
design makes it clear that this is very different
from the beloved original.

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:28.349
Perhaps someone in the show eventually becomes
tired of cutting themselves whenever they

00:03:28.349 --> 00:03:32.560
sit down on what's effectively their office
chair, and commissions the relatively streamlined

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:35.609
and more efficient version we see on Game
of Thrones.

